From: Roy Kaandorp <roykaandorp@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] libcamera: software ISP: contrast based autofocus

The software ISP has no autofocus: the simple pipeline never even opens
the focus lens that the camera sensor discovers as its ancillary device,
so a camera with a perfectly working motor stays at whatever position
the spring rests at.

Give it the classic contrast-detection loop. The CPU stats engine gains
a sharpness statistic -- the summed squared horizontal green gradient of
the pixels it already samples -- and a new Af algorithm sweeps the lens
coarsely across its range, refines around the best position, holds
there, and rescans when the held position has clearly stopped being the
sharp one. The lens is driven from the software ISP on the IPA's
request over a new setLensPosition event, and the lens control range is
handed to the IPA at init time.

AfMode selects between Continuous (the default), Auto with AfTrigger,
and Manual, where LensPosition positions the lens directly; it is
expressed as a 0-1 normalised fraction of the lens travel, as nothing
is known about the optics.

The algorithm only engages for cameras whose tuning file lists Af.

Round two, after use on the device: gather statistics on every frame
rather than every fourth, which quarters the time each autofocus
measurement costs; normalise the sharpness by the mean luminance so a
change of exposure no longer masquerades as a change of focus; and on
refocus look around the current position first, escalating to the full
sweep only when the peak sits at the edge of that window.

